      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ack. I agree that changing to a tri-state wouldn\u0027t allow removing the lock or the local. I was thinking that changing to a Boolean tri-state would make it possible to remove the getApplication() call from the synchronized block, because the value of getApplication() would no longer be necessary to decide whether the proxy should be updated or not. Only the value of mUpdateHttpProxyOnBind would be needed. Basically:\n\n        public void updateHttpProxy() {\n            synchronized (this) {\n                if (mUpdateHttpProxyOnBind \u003d\u003d null) {\n                    // The app is not bound yet. Make a note to update the HTTP proxy when the\n                    // app is bound.\n                    mUpdateHttpProxyOnBind \u003d true;\n                    return;\n                }\n            }\n            // App is present, update the proxy inline.\n            ActivityThread.updateHttpProxy(app);\n        }\n\n...\n...\n      \n    private void handleBindApplication(AppBindData data) {\n        ...\n        ...\n\n            mInitialApplication \u003d app;\n            final boolean updateHttpProxy;\n            synchronized (this) {\n                updateHttpProxy \u003d Boolean.TRUE.equals(mUpdateHttpProxyOnBind);\n                // This synchronized block ensures that any call to updateHttpProxy()\n                // made after the lock is releaseds will see a non-null mInitialApplication.\n                mUpdateHttpProxyOnBind \u003d false;\n            }\n            if (updateHttpProxy) {\n                ActivityThread.updateHttpProxy(app);\n            }\n\n\nThat said, I\u0027m not entirely sure this works. If it does, it seems better because mInitialApplication does not need to be synchronized. That said, you\u0027re correct that it probably _should_ be synchronized because it can be read off-thread.\n\nWhat you have seems fine either way.",
